infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Active vs passive health checks: each sees what the other misses
  desc Use both for defense in depth — neither alone covers every failure mode
  compares
    - label Active — probes on a fixed interval
      children
        - label Probes every 10-30 s
          desc HTTP GET /healthz, TCP SYN, or gRPC check, independent of real client traffic
        - label Ejects after 2-3 consecutive failures
          desc Re-adds after 2-3 consecutive successes
        - label Catches crashed process, network partition, misrouted port
          desc Fast detection even with zero live traffic
        - label Blind to TCP-accepts-but-500
          desc A hung thread pool returning 500 still passes a TCP SYN probe
    - label Passive — observes real traffic
      children
        - label Reacts to live 5xx and timeouts
          desc Envoy outlier detection ejects after 5 consecutive 5xx responses
        - label Ejection 30 s, doubling to ~300 s
          desc Exponential backoff naturally dampens a flapping backend
        - label Catches app hangs, DB pool exhaustion, GC 5xx storms
          desc The silent degradation an HTTP probe can miss if /healthz is a trivial stub
        - label No coverage during low traffic
          desc With no requests there are no observations to flag an outlier
theme
  palette #6366f1 #f43f5e
